<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pagan Min</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pagan Min was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1846 to 1853, the son of Tharrawaddy. His reign was marked by the execution of rivals and arbitrary rule, and a dispute over the treatment of British ship captains at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-burmese-war">Second Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1852, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>. He was deposed by his half-brother Mindon in 1853 and lived on in retirement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pagan-min.jpg" alt="Pagan Min" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archaeological Survey of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomb_of_Bagan_Min%27s_mother.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Maung Biddhu Khyit on 21 June 1811, he was the ninth king of the Konbaung dynasty. His father Tharrawaddy made him Prince of Pagan in August 1842, and he became king when Tharrawaddy died on 17 November 1846. He won the struggle for the succession by having his rival brothers killed, and his chief ministers, Maung Baing Zat and Maung Bhein, enriched themselves by executing wealthy subjects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1851 Maung Ok, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, charged the captains of two British merchant ships with murder, embezzlement and evading customs, fined them 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> and kept them until their debts were paid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>, the governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, sent Commodore George Lambert to demand £920 in compensation and Maung Ok's dismissal, and Pagan Min replaced the governor. On 6 January 1852, after Lambert had seized a Burmese royal ship and the new governor refused to meet a British delegation, British subjects were evacuated and Lambert blockaded the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, which his warships were soon bombarding.</p><p>Pagan Min protested to Dalhousie on 7 February, and on 13 February Dalhousie demanded the equivalent of £100,000 for the cost of preparing for war, to be paid by 1 April. The ultimatum went unanswered, British troops entered Burmese territory and quickly routed the king's forces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> in December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His half-brother Mindon, who opposed the war, fled with his brother Kanaung to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Shwebo</a> and raised a rebellion. After a few weeks of fighting the chief minister Magwe Mingyi went over to Mindon, and Pagan Min gave up the throne to him on 18 February 1853. Mindon spared his life, released the European prisoners and sought peace with the British, though he refused to sign a treaty giving up Burmese territory. Pagan Min died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> during the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">Thibaw</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pagan Min” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mindon (r. 1853–1878), who came to power by deposing his half-brother <b>Pagan Min</b> during the Second Anglo-Burmese War, attempted a programme of modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarapura">Amarapura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.85,96.1&amp;year=1855" aria-label="Amarapura on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pagan Min"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5483 5682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5483 5682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.85,96.1&amp;year=1855">What was here in 1855? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
